<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-separable-eq" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Separable Differential Equations</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    To recall that a <term>first-order differential equation</term> is 
                    one that can be written in the form
                </p>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        \frac{dy}{dx} = F(x,y)
                    </md>
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    To understand that a differential equation is <term>separable</term> if 
                    it can be written in the form
                </p>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        \frac{dy}{dx} = g(x)h(y)
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>
                    and by rewriting the equation in the form
                </p>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        N(y)\,dy = M(x)\,dx
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>
                    the equation can be solved by integrating both sides.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>
    
    <introduction>
        <p>
            We are now in a place where we can begin actually solving some 
            first-order linear differential equations. That is, we're going to 
            begin to learn how to find an <em>actual formula</em> for a solution to the 
            equation, not just approximate it. We also will look at when 
            we can do this because, as we've noted before, we cannot always.
        </p>
        <p>
            We've seen that we can always write a first-order linear differential 
            equation in the form
        </p>
        <p>
            <md>
                \frac{dy}{dx} = F(x,y)
            </md>
        </p>
        <p>
            for some function <m>F</m>. There are a couple of special cases 
            of this format where we can actually get a solution.
        </p>
        <p>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The right-hand side function <m>F(x,y)=g(x)</m>; that 
                        is, there's only <m>x</m>'s on the right-hand side, no 
                        <m>y</m>'s. Then we can <q>multiply</q> both sides of 
                        the equation by <m>dx</m><fn>We continue the custom from 
                        first-semester calculus that <m>dy/dx</m> is not a fraction, 
                        though we treat it like one from then on.</fn> to get
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        <md>
                            \frac{dy}{dx} = g(x) \Longrightarrow dy = g(x)\,dx.
                        </md>
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        We then integrate both sides to get
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        <md>
                            y=\int g(x)\,dx
                        </md>
                    </p>
                    <p>or <m>y=G(x) + C</m>, where <m>G</m> is any antiderivative 
                    of <m>g</m>.</p> 
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The right-hand side function is <m>F(x,y) = h(y)</m>; that 
                        is, there's only <m>y</m>'s on the right-hand side, no 
                        <m>x</m>'s. In this case, we have an autonomous equation and 
                        we could use the methods of the previous section (phase lines, 
                        slope fields) to find good approximations to a solution.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
        <p>
            The issue comes when we do have both <m>x</m>'s and <m>y</m>'s 
            on that right-hand side. The good news is that there are instances 
            where we can fully solve the differential equation. We will explore 
            these instances here and in the next few sections.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-separable-diff-eq">
        <title>Separable Differential Equations</title>
        <p>
            If we can write the right-hand side function <m>F(x,y)</m> as the 
            product of a function of just <m>x</m> times a function of just <m>y</m>, then our 
            differential equation often can be solved.
        </p>
        <definition xml:id="def-separable-diff-eq">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A first-order linear differential equation is called 
                    <term>separable</term> if it can be written in the form
                </p>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        \frac{dy}{dx} = g(x)h(y)
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>
        <example>
            <p>
                The equation <m>y'=xy+2y</m> is separable because we can 
                write it as <m>y'=y(x+2)</m> with <m>g(x)=x+2</m> and 
                <m>h(y) = y</m>.
            </p>
        </example>
        <example>
            <p>
                The equation <m>y'=y+\cos x</m> is <em>not</em> separable 
                because it cannot be factored into the product of a function 
                of just <m>x</m> and a function of just <m>y</m>.
            </p>
        </example>
        <example>
            <p>
                The equation <m>y'=y^2+y+3</m> is separable with <m>g(x)=1</m> 
                and <m>h(y)=y^2+y+3</m>. This equation is also autonomous. In 
                fact, <alert>all autonomous equations are also separable</alert>.
            </p>
        </example>
        <example>
            <p>
                The equation <m>y'=x+1</m> is separable. (It falls into our 
                first special case above. <em>Any equation where the 
                right-hand side only has one variable is separable.</em>)
            </p>
            <p>
                We can in fact solve this particular equation:
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    y = \int (x+1)\,dx = \frac{x^2}{2} + x + C
                </md>
                Whether we can solve this type of differential equation (or 
                an autonomous equation) depends on the integrability of the 
                right-hand side. For instance, the differential equation 
                <m>y'=e^{x^2}</m> is separable. However, <m>e^{x^2}</m> has 
                no antiderivative. If we had an initial-value problem that 
                involved this equation, we would have to approximate the 
                integral of <m>e^{x^2}</m> using numerical methods.
            </p>
        </example>
        <p>
            When we have a separable equation, we can mostly solve it using 
            the <term><q>separate and integrate</q></term> method. We first 
            <em>separate the variables</em> -- all the terms with <m>y</m>'s 
            in them go to the left side, and all the terms with <m>x</m>'s 
            in them go the the right side. We then <em>integrate</em> both sides 
            (assuming we can do so) which leads to an <term>implicit solution</term> 
            of the equation. That is, we obtain an equation which gives the 
            relationship between <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>. In many cases, we can 
            solve this equation for <m>y</m>. If we can, we have an <term>explicit 
            solution</term>, an actual formula for the dependent variable <m>y</m> 
            in terms of the independent variable <m>x</m>. Obtaining an explicit 
            solution is always the goal, though it's not always possible.
        </p>
        <example>
            <p>
                Suppose that we wish to solve the differential equation 
                <m>y'=-xy^2</m>. This equation is separable with <m>g(x)
                = x</m> and <m>h(y)=-y^2</m>. (It doesn't matter where we 
                put the minus sign since we can move it as needed.) We first 
                rewrite the left-hand side in Leibniz notation:
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    \frac{dy}{dx} = -xy^2.
                </md>
                
            </p>
            <p>
                We then separate the variables, moving the <m>y</m> pieces 
                to the left and the <m>x</m> pieces to the right, remembering 
                that the differentials <m>dx</m> and <m>dy</m> <em>must be 
                in the numerators of any fractions that result</em>. Thus we 
                divide both sides by <m>-y^2</m> and multiply both sides by 
                <m>dx</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>
                   - \frac{dy}{y^2} = x\,dx
                </md>
                We rewrite this in the forms:
                <md>
                   <mrow> -\frac1{y^2}\,dy \amp = x\,dx</mrow>  
                   <mrow> -y^{-2}\,dy\amp = x\,dx </mrow>
                </md>
                We can now integrate both sides with respect to the separate 
                variables to get
                <md>
                    <mrow>-\frac{y^{-1}}{-1} \amp = \frac{x^2}{2} + C</mrow>
                    <mrow>\frac1{y} \amp = \frac{x^2}{2} + C</mrow>
                </md>
                This last equation is an <em>implicit solution</em> to our 
                differential equation. <alert>Notice that we put a single constant 
                of integration on the right-hand side</alert>. In this case, we can 
                get an <em>explicit solution</em> by taking the reciprocal 
                of both sides.
                <md>
                    \boxed{y = \frac1{\frac{x^2}{2}+C}}
                </md>
            </p>
            <aside>
                <p>
                    Note in the last step that we must take the reciprocal of the entire right-
                    hand side at once. The answer is <em>not</em> 
                    <m>y=\frac2{x^2} + \frac1{C}</m>. Not at all.
                </p>
            </aside>
        </example>
    </subsection>
</section>